== Abstract ==

This document specifies getblocktemplate changes for the [[bip-blake2b-pow.mediawiki|BLAKE2b upgrade]].

== Copyright ==

This document is licensed under the 2-clause BSD license.

== Motivation ==

The BLAKE2b upgrade changes the block header and the proof-of-work algorithm.
Miners using getblocktemplate need the additional template data to construct version 2 block headers.

== Specification ==

=== getblocktemplate ===

A client supporting this BIP SHOULD include the rule string <code>blake2b</code> in the <code>rules</code> list of its getblocktemplate request, both before and after activation.
A template server MAY reject a request that does not include <code>blake2b</code>.

A template for a block at or after the activation height is a ''BLAKE2b template''.
The <code>rules</code> list of a BLAKE2b template MUST include the rule string:

<pre>
!blake2b
</pre>

The rule strings <code>blake2b</code> and <code>!blake2b</code> imply every rule string of a deployment active before the activation height, except <code>reduced_data</code>.
A template server SHOULD NOT reject a request that includes <code>blake2b</code> for lacking a rule string it implies, and a BLAKE2b template MAY omit any rule string that <code>!blake2b</code> implies.

The template's <code>version</code> field MUST contain the complete 32-bit version, including the header-version high bit.

At the activation height, the template's <code>coinbaseaux</code> object MUST include:

<pre>
blake2b_headline
</pre>

whose value is the exact activation headline bytes encoded as hex.

=== Version 2 header construction ===

The existing <code>height</code> field supplies the committed block height.
The client derives the committed transaction count from the final block it constructs, including the generation transaction;
no separate template field supplies this count.

The <code>curtime</code> and <code>mintime</code> fields refer to the block time used by the consensus rules, rather than the serialized timestamp before adding any enabled time offset.

When applicable, the one-time activation target adjustment is already included in the <code>bits</code> field.
It is also included in any BIP 23 <code>target</code> or <code>fulltarget</code> field that specifies the network target.
Clients MUST NOT apply that adjustment again.

The client selects the nonce, extranonce, hashing-hardware profile, time offset and its enable bit, merge-mining right-hand side, XOR key and XOR-mask clear-bit count under the BLAKE2b proof-of-work rules.

Where BIP 23 submission abbreviations and merkle-only shares refer to the 80-byte block header, a block using the version 2 header uses the complete version 2 header instead.

=== Deprecated fields ===

BIP 23 provided the <code>noncerange</code> field only for parity with getwork, and never required clients or servers to support it.
This BIP formally deprecates it for BLAKE2b templates:
a server SHOULD omit it;
if present, it MUST be <code>00000000ffffffff</code>, the full range of the first 32 bits of the nonce.
Clients MAY ignore it.

This BIP also deprecates the BIP 23 <code>nonces</code> request key.
A client that includes <code>blake2b</code> in its supported rules SHOULD NOT send it.

== Rationale ==

'''Why prefix the blake2b rule with "!"?'''

No client unaware of this BIP can mine a BLAKE2b template.
The <code>!</code> prefix, under BIP 8, makes such a client refuse the template with a clear error, rather than wasting work on blocks that can never be valid.

'''Why may servers require the blake2b rule?'''

The <code>!blake2b</code> rule in the template already tells a client implementing BIP 8 not to use the template unless it supports this BIP, so a server need not require the rule in requests.
Unlike segwit, for which BIP 145 lets a server serve clients lacking the rule a template without witness data, no template after activation can be mined without BLAKE2b, so knowing in advance whether a client supports this BIP gives the server no choice of template to make.
A server typically already requires some rule in requests, such as <code>segwit</code>, to be sure the client handles rules prefixed with <code>!</code>.
Since <code>blake2b</code> implies the earlier rules, requiring it alone is simpler than accepting either it or <code>segwit</code>.

'''Why does the blake2b rule imply earlier rules?'''

A client supporting this BIP necessarily postdates the earlier deployments, and could not mine a valid block after activation without handling them, so listing them as well conveys nothing.
The <code>reduced_data</code> rule is excluded because it is temporary, so its presence still tells the client whether it applies to the template's block.

'''Why deprecate noncerange rather than extend it to the whole nonce?'''

BIP 23 included <code>noncerange</code> only so that getblocktemplate would be a complete superset of getwork, under which a pool could divide the 32-bit historical nonce among clients working on the same work.
getwork dates from CPU and GPU mining, when searching 2<sup>32</sup> nonces could take several minutes, longer than a job remained current.
The field cannot describe the 96-bit nonce of the version 2 header.
Modern hardware searches 2<sup>32</sup> nonces in a fraction of a second, so it always needs more work than the historical nonce can provide, and dividing that nonce among clients no longer makes sense.
The 96-bit nonce, however, is large enough that dividing it would make sense:
even if a server fixed its first 32 bits, 2<sup>64</sup> nonces would remain, enough for about 307 Ph/s for a full minute.
It is nonetheless not divided, because the nonce belongs to the mining hardware, which divides it internally among its chips and cores, and a server constraining it would work against that division.
A server may instead divide the 128-bit extranonce in the version 2 header among clients, as specified in the [[bip-blake2b-gbt-pooled.mediawiki|BLAKE2b pooled-mining BIP]].
The full range remains permitted so that existing servers, which always report it, need not change.

'''Why deprecate nonces?'''

The <code>nonces</code> key requests a nonce range of a given size, which only <code>noncerange</code> could provide.
The whole 96-bit nonce of the version 2 header is always available to the client, so only counts above 2<sup>96</sup> would remain meaningful.
Although JSON places no limit on the size of a Number, many JSON implementations cannot represent integers that large exactly, and some cannot parse JSON containing them at all.
The [[bip-blake2b-gbt-pooled.mediawiki|BLAKE2b pooled-mining BIP]] instead divides the 128-bit extranonce, with a new request key giving the number of extranonce bytes the client needs.

== Backward compatibility ==

Clients unaware of this BIP cannot mine BLAKE2b templates.
Those implementing BIP 8 will not attempt to use them, because of the <code>!blake2b</code> rule.

== Reference implementation ==

The reference implementation is Bitcoin Knots tag [https://github.com/bitcoinknots/bitcoin/commit/8c85b1585dac23f964e2dd32045624de7f02aa58 v29.4.1.knots20260508], principally <code>src/rpc/mining.cpp</code>.

Bitcoin Knots pull request [https://github.com/bitcoinknots/bitcoin/pull/518 #518] proposes dropping <code>noncerange</code> from getblocktemplate.
Pull request [https://github.com/bitcoinknots/bitcoin/pull/519 #519] proposes accepting <code>blake2b</code> in a request without <code>segwit</code>.

Exception:
the <code>blake2b_headline</code> field in <code>coinbaseaux</code> is implemented in [https://github.com/bitcoinknots/bitcoin/commit/dc82be77dd741dfa63e1f816367b15364d55b051 v29.4.1.knots20260508rc4], and was removed before the final release.
Released versions omit it from getblocktemplate, as the activation block it applies to has already been mined.
